name sat-10-duel-merge-1
timeout 520
time 1991-01-19T08:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# MEASURING RIG, NOT A CAMPAIGN RUNG. `sat-*` files belong to no `.fbc`, are named by no campaign and
# move no doctrine. Format: doc/missions/syntax.md; the criteria: doc/doctrine-evolution.md 4.2.
#
# WHAT IS NEW AGAINST `sat-07-dry-merge`, WHICH THIS FILE IS THE FLIGHT-IDENTICAL TWIN OF: BOTH SIDES
# ARE GRADED. `sat-07` gives its eight MiG-29 one `survive until 300` each, and [MESS, E19 3 and
# reproduced here by a second instrument] their outcome class is then (24, 8) on EVERY allele of their
# whole strategy space — a constant, and a search cannot climb a constant. That is E-30. This file adds
# a rung ladder to EACH side and changes no coordinate, no store and no brief.
#
# THE ADDITION COSTS NO PHYSICS, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED: an `objective` is read by the
# judge and by nobody else (`core/FBZone.h` is RESTRICTED, `FBMissionMonitor` holds its own plan copy),
# so against `sat-07` under the same binary 17 of 18 telemetry files are BYTE-IDENTICAL by SHA-256 and
# the eighteenth (`ma1`) differs only in `zone_zb_s`/`zone_zb_in` AFTER t = 300.1 — the two columns the
# judge itself writes, at the instant `sat-07`'s MiG concluded and this file's has not. The aircraft
# flew the same flight. What changes is the VERDICT: the eight MiG-29 go SUCCESS -> TIMEOUT, i.e. red's
# V drops 24 -> 16 and its M becomes a count instead of a constant.
#
# THE MARGIN, IN THE HEAD, AS A NUMBER, AND NOW ONCE PER SIDE. Every graded quantity here is either a
# declared-cylinder DWELL or a PLANAR MIN-RANGE between two named units; every rung sits in a MEASURED
# gap of its own quantity's lever spectrum, and every gap is quoted against the chaos amplitude of THAT
# quantity over the +-3 m spawn grid — taken as the WORSE of the two perturbations, one's own and the
# opponent's, because a rung has to survive either.
#
#   RED  narrowest 14.5x — `ma3`'s min-range to `sw3`, 77.9 m against a chaos amplitude of 5.38 m.
#        Widest 29.0x. Eight ladders, 47 rungs, one per MiG-29, each against ONE named F-16.
#   BLUE narrowest 12.3x — `sw2`'s `zb` dwell, 3.70 s against 0.300 s. The four min-range ladders added
#        this round run 17.9 / 31.4 / 55.6 / 178.7x. The `zs` ladders and two of the `zb` rungs have a
#        chaos amplitude of 0.000 s over the whole grid, so their ratio is unbounded and is reported as
#        an absolute distance instead: 14.3 / 21.2 / 61.5 / 98.0 / 143.6 / 146.4 s.
#
# AND A SECOND CONDITION ON THE BLUE RUNGS, WHICH THIS ROUND HAD TO MEASURE ITS WAY INTO: a rung must
# clear the chaos band of its own quantity under EVERY DECLARED OPPONENT, not just the committed one.
# The first placement here obeyed `E18`'s rule to the letter — 17x against the committed MiG — and
# blue's own class still flipped **4 of 8** under `red emcon-hi` and **3 of 8** under `red sort-far`,
# because a different red allele moves blue's min-range onto the rung. The rungs above are placed
# outside [min - 10c, max + 10c] of blue's own S7 population for all ten red alleles, which is X-17
# carried from the SCREEN into the PLACEMENT, and the screen then comes back 0 of 8 on all ten pairs.
#
# WHY BLUE WAS RE-LADDERED AT ALL, and it is a finding rather than a tuning: `E20`'s emission seam
# (`duels.md` D3e) moved every MiG-29 that is briefed a GCI range beyond its own 50 km reach, and this
# geometry is one of them. Measured with the two binaries side by side on `sat-07` ITSELF, same
# instrument, same lever file: blue went from **9 distinct classes, 60.0 % modal, 10 movers** (which is
# `E18` 3's committed table, reproduced to the digit) to **5 distinct, 68.0 %, 8 movers** — S1 REFUSED.
# The lost movers are `shape-wide`, `emcon-wide` and `shape-flat`. The four min-range ladders make them
# visible again; the arithmetic is `E18` 3's own: 12 of the 24 levers are the identity map on any F-16
# side, so 12 movers is the CEILING and 13 of 25 = 52.0 % the best modal share this lever file allows.
# This file reaches both.
#
# THE SCRAMBLE, on both sides: no ladder names the unit a briefed sort contract would hand its owner,
# and the eight red ladders spread over all four sweepers (two each) while the four blue ladders name
# four different MiG-29 out of eight. A fighter that closes on the WRONG opponent scores nothing, which
# is what stops "fly at whatever is nearest" from being the answer to every rung.
#
# WHAT REMOVES THE COIN, unchanged from `sat-07` and quoted because this file inherits it: THE
# ENGAGEMENT IS DRY. Both sides carry `brief_master_arm sim`, the hardware refuses every launch — [MESS]
# 63 `sms RELEASE_REJECTED reason=hardware_precedence`, 0 `damage KILL`, 0 `monitor KO` — so sorting,
# locking, cranking, the RWR answer and the emission discipline all run and only the discrete event that
# amplifies a 3 m perturbation is gone.
#
# GENE FAMILIES, MEASURED (tools/fb_duel_arena.py, `--elev const`):
#   BLUE, 24 levers of tools/levers-campaign-g5.txt: BASELINE (15, 23), 12 distinct classes, modal
#     share 52.0 % (13 of 25), S7 0 of 8 on all ten (cell, red) pairs.
#     live  G1 shape 6 of 6, G5 emcon 3 of 3, G3 net 1 of 2 (`net-off`), G7 ccip 1 of 2, G6 bias 1 of 3
#           (`bias-rail` only — 2.3 km of track, i.e. no delivery at all, NOT a direction anybody may
#           quote), 12 movers of 24, which is the ceiling
#     INERT G2 cover 0 of 3 (the AIM-120 binds 0.3 s — by AIRFRAME), G4 energy 0 of 3 (it lives inside
#           `Phase::Bfm` and no unit here flies `set task bfm`)
#   RED, 9 levers of tools/levers-red-mig29.txt (the alphabet a MiG-29 can express at all, E-31):
#     BASELINE (16, 24), 8 distinct classes, modal share 30.0 % (3 of 10), S7 0 of 8 on all 25
#     (cell, blue) pairs.
#     live  G5 emcon 3 of 5, G3 sort 4 of 4 — 7 movers of 9, 6 of them off their band's rail
#     INERT `emcon-tight` (0.1) and `emcon-mid` (0.4): the PUR-31 gate is `f x 27.0 nm` against briefed
#           GCI calls of 120/90/60 km, so both sample the same side of every threshold. That is a
#           property of the LEVER FILE against this rig and it is stated rather than hidden.
#
# AND THE CLOCK, because X-7 is the trap every rig here is built against: the run duration is 520.0 s
# on every lever of both sides — one value, no spread. `sat-07`'s second value (300.1 s, where every
# unit had concluded) is gone precisely because red now carries objectives it does not meet.
#
# THE ARENA: four F-16 sweeping east against eight MiG-29 under a GCI brief, plus a CCIP pair on two
# soft aim points. `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4). `wx fixture` is the committed GFS
# blob. Day, 11:00 local. Every coordinate is [SET] except the two aim points, which sit at the fixed
# point of their own release lattice (`sat-07`'s converged iteration, unchanged).
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION, AND IT IS NOW ASKED OF BOTH SEATS: IN A MERGE WHERE NOBODY CAN SHOOT, DOES
# EACH SIDE'S SHAPE AND EMISSION POLICY DECIDE HOW CLOSE IT GETS TO THE OPPONENT IT WAS NAMED FOR?
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   events.log         `mission WINDOW_CLOSED` at t = 300.1, one line per declared span; `mission
#                      IDENTIFIED`; `zone ENTER`/`EXIT`; `sms RELEASE_REJECTED` (the dry proof)
#   telemetry_sw*.csv  `zone_zb_s`, `zone_zs_s`, `flt_src`, `flt_assign`, `flt_switch`
#
# READING RULE, BINDING: exit 3 = TIMEOUT is the ONLY exit this file produces and is NOT the
# measurement. THE MEASUREMENT IS TWO OUTCOME CLASSES, (V, M) summed over the six F-16 and (V, M)
# summed over the eight MiG-29, AND THE SPREAD OF EACH ACROSS ITS OWN LEVER SET. They are never
# compared with each other: two airframes with different objectives make a cross-side comparison a
# measurement of the seat (`fb_fitness.match_points`). A single run of this file measures NOTHING.


zone zb 33.10000 44.50000 10000 0 12000
zone zs 33.20000 44.50000 60000 6600 9000

